Ohle Claussen <claussen@dpi.physik.uni-goettingen.de> writes:
> On 2006-05-29, Getsome wrote:
> > I got a samarai and am trying to figure out how to #twoweapon
> > every time I try I end up wielding my pickax or I am left empty handed

> issuing the command. To do this, change to your off-hand by perssing 'x', then
> wield a weapon (with 'w'). Change back again to wield your primary weapon,
> then #twoweapon.

> If you have the "pushweapon" option enabled, the sequence gets shorter: Press
> 'w', wield secondary, press 'w' again, wield primary, #twoweapon.

Pushweapon doesn't necessarily make the sequence shorter for every time
after using pick-axe. Without pushweapon, your secondary weapon STAYS in
the secondary position after you once wield it and change secondary with
x, so after that every time after pick-axing you just need to 'w' the
primary weapon and then #twoweapon.

With pushweapon, you'll have to first wield the secondary weapon EVERY
time before wielding the primary one. (Unless, of course, you have the
pick-axe as secondary, in which case you just use x and #twoweapon.)

Of course, if you often WANT to change the secondary weapon, then
pushweapon makes it faster. But if you prefer keeping the same secondary
weapon for a longer time, then it just means extra shuffling.

